It might sound weird, but CAPCOM is like my third parent. Or maybe the second, because my daddy "went to get some milk and never came back" anyway. People working for this company and their creations supported me in my childhood, helped me get through my teens, now welcomed me back as an adult many years later, and it still feels the same. 

[i]By "the same" I mean that they were still able to turn me into a little kid screaming in horror, but it's okay. It's part of the deal.[/i]

Let's briefly go over some things that RE2 Remake is:

– it's gorgeous; shooting zombies has never felt so satisfying, and the number of little details is outright insane;
– it's frightening; you might land 6 headshots in a row, but the staggering bastard will keep walking towards you, making you feel helpless and very limited in your options, which is a necessary ingredient in every successful horror game;
– it's riveting; despite the nightmare that you go through every time you load a save, it's rather difficult to stop and return to your mundane duties;
– it's a bit shameless; the original music should have been included in the game by default, not sold as a paid DLC, since there was no extra work involved in creating it – not cool, but I guess everyone's got to make a living somehow;
– it's faithful to the original and very creative at the same time, which I consider to be the ultimate advantage.

P.S. Make your GF play it. It will be a lot of fun (for you)

A very good [b]REmake[/b] (get it?) of Resident Evil 2, brought back to life thanks to the RE Engine.
This game is wonderful, polished, and as far as I experienced absent of a lot of bugs and full of speedrun exploits. 
Including: 
-A Hardcore mode made just to drain your ammo (regular zombies take up to FIFTEEN gun shots to kill unless they get decapitated, which is a cool thing for the first hour, but will make you question why are you using a gun in the first place;
-A very angry and tall boi in a coat gently following you around to make sure you don't get hurt and [b]gently caresses you[/b] when you feel sad and tense;
-Red puppies with a long tongue that they use to kiss you;
-Depending on the scenario, a charming asian with a red dress or a little girl with daddy issues (literally);
-A scientist literally [i] too angry to die[/i]
-A plethura of weapons to satisfy your need for destruction..
-...and very little ammo just to destroy your dreams;
-Realistically very unlikely puzzles ([i]who the hell builds a puzzle with chess pieces [b]in the ♥♥♥♥♥♥♥ sewers[/b]??[/i])
-Lepers who just want to nibble at you because they're envious of your skin care;

A fun, lighthearted experience for the whole family.
10/10 would get slapped by X again

- Just picked up a magnum;
- Arklay Sheriff outfit is sitting in chest;
- Genre of game changed from survival horror to I don't know.  I hear Nebraska's nice.

A game that brig back the core of Resident Evil franchise with a new gameplay. It gave me the same feeling that I had when I played 1996 Bio Hazard version and all pleasure by exploring the 1998 Bio Hazard 2. A great game with a great gameplay with a great remade story. 

9 out a 10 score.

I took 1 point because it has 3 problems: 
- Zombie Grab Box is bad. If the zombie touch the tip of a finger, the grab-cutscene starts;
- The Scenario B beginning is missing;
- The needs to get the rewards are exaggerated.

The game is AWESOME! For real, this is what all of fans had waited for! It is when an old game wrapped in a new, beautiful cover gives a great feelings of returning to your childhood. 2 stories to choose, 2 charming heroes - Leon and Claire, still young and naive, are once again plunging into the zombie epidemic of Raccoon City. You will visit these familiar locations - the police station, sewer area and, of course, the most secret laboratory of Umbrella's. But now these places had greatly expanded and became more dangerous.
[u]Main +:[/u]
+ old good atmosphere of fear;
+ zombies are more deadly, persistent and unpredictable;
+ mr. X is the second Jack Baker and will chase you anytime and anywhere (you will always hear his loud, heavy steps);
+ new system of damages: every bullet can leave a mark in the flesh of zombies, that looks very great and realistic;
+ moderately recycled familiar locations.
[u]Main -:[/u]
- only a few in-game OST (sad but you can buy an original of 98's and that's +200 to atmosphere and nostalgia feelings for sure);
- a bit differences between scenarios and plots mismatches (like in original game).

In general - the game meet all expectations and deserves an 9 / 10.

Now we really want... guess what?[spoiler]STAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAARS! :D[/spoiler]
And also the splendid Code Veronica.
Love you Capcom, cheers! :)
